    "parsed": {
      "report_date": "2026-11-08",
      "title": "SSHole (CVE-2026-123158) Activity 2026",
      "summary": "SSHole (CVE-2026-123158) is a flaw in OpenSSH on musl-based Linux such as Alpine that lets an oversized password authenticate to accounts configured to reject password logins. Working exploits were publicly available, and CISA added the CVE to its KEV catalog two days after the first successful exploitation was observed. GreyNoise saw scanning and exploitation, with successful exploitation tracked as executing shell commands on vulnerable systems. One attacker cluster used the exploit to reconnaissance systems, add SSH keys, create accounts, and deploy a Python-based agent named TAC Agent that uses an HTTPS C2 server. The operator also used a shared enrollment token and ran shell commands through the agent, with activity reported across multiple countries. Defenders should patch OpenSSH and review systems for unauthorized SSH keys and accounts.",
      "completeness": "Medium",
      "threats": [
        "Sshole_vuln",
        "Tacagent"
